//? Title: Proximal Gradient and FISTA: Loss, Thresholds and Stationarity
//? Subtitle: Exact soft thresholding before a matched Iris lasso comparison
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A regression loss with an L1 penalty is convex but nonsmooth at zero coefficients.
Ordinary smooth-gradient stationarity is therefore the wrong stopping target.
We derive the proximal update and its soft-threshold map, check a two-coordinate
solution, and show a rate that is stable for plain gradient steps but unstable for
acceleration. Iris then supplies a matched raw-unit objective, compared with the
independent coordinate-descent Lasso implementation across penalties.
)
\toc

> The method

Write F=f+g, where f has a Lipschitz gradient and g has an accessible proximal map.
A quadratic upper model of f gives the proximal-gradient step. For the L1 penalty,
each penalised coordinate has the soft-threshold solution:

$$
 x^+=\operatorname{prox}_{\eta g}(x-\eta\nabla f(x)),\qquad
 \operatorname{prox}_{\eta\lambda|\cdot|}(z)
 =\operatorname{sign}(z)\max(|z|-\eta\lambda,0).
$$
\alttext(A smooth-gradient step is followed by the proximal map. For L1, subtract eta times lambda from each magnitude and clamp at zero while retaining its sign.)

The threshold follows by solving z-w+eta*lambda*sign(z)=0 in the positive and
negative regions and checking the zero subgradient interval. An unpenalised
intercept passes through unchanged. The library interface's gradient supplies
only the smooth part; evaluate returns the entire composite objective.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_proximal_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_proximal_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_proximal_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_proximal_mechanism.svg build/opt_proximal_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_proximal_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_proximal_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The proximal map handles the nonsmooth term while a mapping norm checks the whole objective.)
\alttext(A smooth-gradient trial is soft thresholded. An unpenalised intercept is left unchanged. The proximal-gradient mapping, rather than the raw smooth gradient, diagnoses composite stationarity.)

ISTA repeats that map. FISTA instead evaluates gradients at extrapolated y points,
uses t_next=(1+sqrt(1+4*t squared))/two and
 y_next=x_next+(t-1)/t_next*(x_next-x). With a convex f, convex g and eta<=1/L,
the standard accepted-iterate objective bounds are O(1/k) for ISTA and O(1/k squared)
for FISTA. Those bounds do not assert that FISTA's extrapolated-point objective
is monotone. This implementation has no backtracking, adaptive restart or monotone
safeguard. Both stop on coordinate displacement; the post-run mapping is

$$
 G_\eta(x)=\frac{x-\operatorname{prox}_{\eta g}(x-\eta\nabla f(x))}{\eta}.
$$
\alttext(The proximal-gradient mapping is the displacement from the current point to one complete proximal-gradient update, divided by the step. Its zero norm expresses composite first-order stationarity.)

For a convex problem, G_eta=zero is an optimality condition. A small coordinate
change with a tiny rate need not imply a small mapping. Neither solver requests
full objective values inside its loop: the recorder samples gradient-request
points afterwards, and the final returned point is checked separately. Dense
least-squares gradient cost is O(n*p), with O(p) optimizer state beyond the design.

> Small worked example

>> A two-coordinate lasso solution and its threshold map

Let f=.25*((x0-3)^2+(x1+1)^2) and g=.5*(abs(x0)+abs(x1)). The exact solution is
(2,0), with F=1.5. At this point the smooth gradient is (-.5,.5), not zero, but
soft thresholding x-gradient(x) at .5 returns x unchanged. From the origin with
eta one, ISTA's first point is (1,0) and after k steps x0=2*(1-2 to the -k),
while x1 remains zero. The returned four-step point is (1.875,0). Gradient-request
samples describe pre-update points for ISTA and look-ahead points for FISTA.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_proximal_threshold.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::L1LeastSquaresObjective f({{1, 0}, {0, 1}}, {3, -1}, .5);
  const auto a = o::trace_optimize<o::ProximalFunction>(f, o::ProximalGradient({1, 4, 0}), {0, 0}),
             b = o::trace_optimize<o::ProximalFunction>(f, o::FISTA({1, 4, 0}), {0, 0});
  std::vector<double> z = dm::seql(-3, 3, 201).values(), threshold;
  for (double v : z)
    threshold.push_back(f.proximal({v, 0}, 1)[0]);
  auto map = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .line(z, z, "identity", {130, 130, 130})
                 .line(z, threshold, "soft threshold", {40, 100, 190})
                 .title("Threshold magnitude 0.5")
                 .x_label("input coordinate")
                 .y_label("proximal output")
                 .x_limits(-3, 3)
                 .y_limits(-3, 4.5)
                 .legend({"identity", "proximal map"}, {{130, 130, 130}, {40, 100, 190}});
  auto loss = o::optimization_comparison(f, {a, b}, {"ISTA", "FISTA"}, "gradient", 1.5)
                  .title("Requested gradient points, four steps");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(map).add(loss).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/opt_proximal_threshold.svg");
  std::print(
      "ISTA\n\n{}\n\nFISTA\n\n{}\n\nExact optimum: F={:.6g}, smooth-gradient norm={:.6g}, mapping "
      "norm={:.6g}.\n",
      a.summary().to_md(7),
      b.summary().to_md(7),
      f.evaluate({2, 0}),
      std::hypot(f.gradient({2, 0})[0], f.gradient({2, 0})[1]),
      o::proximal_mapping_norm(f, {2, 0}, 1));
}
```
