\caption(Soft thresholding gives exact zeros and a correct stationarity condition for the composite objective.)
\alttext(The L1 proximal map has a flat zero region and shifted linear tails. Requested-point losses for four ISTA/FISTA steps are shown separately from the exact returned-point checks.)

> Checking the rate assumption

>> A rate stable for ISTA need not be stable for FISTA

Use the smooth special case F=.5*x squared, lambda=zero and L=one. Plain ISTA is
gradient descent, so eta=1.5 still contracts by -0.5. FISTA's extrapolation can be
unstable at that same rate, which violates the standard eta<=1/L assumption.
Compare eta=.5 and 1.5 over thirty updates from x=one. The display retains the
actual requested-point losses; no acceleration theorem is asserted outside its
rate assumptions, and a finite budget is not called convergence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_proximal_rates.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::L1LeastSquaresObjective      f({{1}}, {0}, 0);
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  const std::vector<std::string>  labels{"ISTA .5", "FISTA .5", "ISTA 1.5", "FISTA 1.5"};
  for (double rate : {.5, 1.5}) {
    runs.push_back(
        o::trace_optimize<o::ProximalFunction>(f, o::ProximalGradient({rate, 30, 0}), {1}));
    runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::ProximalFunction>(f, o::FISTA({rate, 30, 0}), {1}));
  }
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, labels, "gradient")
      .title("A fixed-rate acceleration counterexample")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_proximal_rates.svg");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i)
    std::print(
        "{}\n\n{}\n\nMapping norm: {:.6g}.\n\n",
        labels[i],
        runs[i].summary().to_md(7),
        o::proximal_mapping_norm(f, runs[i].coordinates, .5));
}
```
