// result_end
\caption(Penalty paths compare matched optima while retaining finite-budget coefficient and stationarity errors.)
\alttext(Three raw-unit slope paths show FISTA lines with coordinate-descent control points. A second panel shows the maximum coefficient difference for each declared penalty.)

> Conclusions and references

The exact threshold map and two-coordinate optimum show why the smooth gradient
can remain nonzero at a correct lasso solution. The rate counterexample qualifies
acceleration claims, and the Iris analysis matches scaling, intercept and penalty
before comparing numerical solutions. A small displacement or a flat requested-point
loss curve is supplemented by the proximal mapping norm. These training-objective
checks do not estimate predictive generalisation or regularisation-selection uncertainty.

- A. Beck and M. Teboulle (2009), [A Fast Iterative Shrinkage-Thresholding Algorithm for Linear Inverse Problems](https://doi.org/10.1137/080716542).
- J. Friedman, T. Hastie and R. Tibshirani (2010), [Regularization Paths for Generalized Linear Models via Coordinate Descent](https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v033.i01), the separate reference solver family.

> Reading the numerical evidence

`trace_optimize` calls the configured native optimizer through its stated abstract
interface. It records callback requests without inserting fitting evaluations.
Value calls can be rejected trials; gradient calls can be look-ahead points.
`optimization_comparison` explicitly re-evaluates selected points afterwards on
these pure objectives. Those diagnostic calls are excluded from fitting counts.
Callback indices are neither accepted iterations nor wall time. The plotted gap
uses the declared reference (minimum or stated lower bound) and a display floor of 1e-14; that floor does
not modify any optimizer update. Returned coordinates, checked objective and
appropriate stationarity/feasibility checks carry more information than a return
from a finite iteration budget. Native APIs do not expose a uniform stop-status flag.
The recorder retains exceptions and rejects nonfinite requested values/gradients;
this makes failures visible rather than letting an instrumented run continue on NaN.

